# `for (let k = 0; k < from.length; k++) list.push(from[k])`. The push that has to grow `list` leaves the fast loop for one turn, and the loop is entered again behind that turn.
# The loop leaves `from` alone: its length is read once, in front of everything, so no turn reads where the elements of `from` are to ask `k < from.length`.
# That is read where the fast loop is entered, at first and behind a turn that grew `list`, and nothing there compares it with an earlier reading. No turn of the fast loop reads it.
